==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|bonny}}
* {{l|en|bonie}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|boni}}, probably from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|bon}}, feminine {{m|fro|bonne||[[good]]}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|bonus||good}}. See {{m|en|bounty}}, and compare {{m|en|bonus}}, {{m|en|boon}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[gay|Gay]]; [[merry]]; [[frolicsome]]; [[cheerful]]; [[blithe]].
#* [[w:Shakespeare|Shakespeare]]
#*: ''Be you blithe and '''bonny'''''.
#* Sir Walter [[Scott]]
#*: ''Report speaks you a '''bonny''' monk, that would hear the matin chime ere he quitted his bowl.''
# {{lb|en|Geordie}} [[beautiful|Beautiful]]; [[pretty]]; [[attractive]].
# {{lb|en|dialectal|Scotland|Northern England}} [[fine]] [[good]] (often used ironically)
#: {{ux|en|My '''bonnie''' friend, come over here.}}

